  <h2>Context</h2>
  <p>
    Student work from 2023, kept public alongside
    <a href="/work/bike-theft-tracker">bike-theft-tracker</a> as the honest start
    of the trajectory.
  </p>

  <h2>What it does</h2>
  <p>
    A task board with four workflow columns. Tasks move between columns, each task
    has its own route so a specific card can be linked to directly, and the board
    survives a page reload through local persistence.
  </p>

  <h2>What I learned from it</h2>
  <p>
    This is the project where a few React ideas stopped being things I had read
    about:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Where state lives determines your component tree.</strong> I started
      by putting state wherever it was convenient and ended up with props threaded
      through components that did not care about them. Lifting state to the lowest
      common ancestor is a rule that only makes sense after you have violated it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Routing per task changes the data model.</strong> Once a task has a
      URL, it needs a stable identity, and the board can no longer be an array you
      reorder freely. That constraint arrived late and forced a rewrite.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Persistence exposes every assumption.</strong> Serialising to
      localStorage immediately surfaced state that was not actually serialisable,
      and state that should never have been stored at all.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Status</h2>
  <p>Archived.</p>
</Work>
